   "From that of Your Paternity I understood that for now there was no occasion to send workmen to the Indies, and therefore there was more need of prayers than of multiplying letters. As to the first, I say that whatever efficacy the prayers of a sinner have before his Divine Majesty, the same have mine — recognizing myself quite unworthy of such grace; but trusting in his infinite mercy, I hope that it will be to console me, and to give occasion to Your Paternity to be able to employ her sons in the aid of those souls. As to the second — that is, of not multiplying letters — I confess the truth: in this I seem to myself to proceed not as a free cause, but a necessary one; for so great is the desire I have that, if I do not find myself already arrived — not to say departed — I shall never cease to supplicate and pray her. Therefore I pray her to have patience, and to bear with one who is indiscreet not because he wishes, but because for his consolation he finds no other means. Father Luigi della Torre wrote to me that, when opportunity of departure occurred, he hoped to make me be one among the others; I would wish that Your Paternity confirm all this with her authority. The principal cause that moved me to this request is that, notified once by Your Paternity, besides that I would be sure of departure, I would have in my judgment more obligation to attend to the acquisition of those virtues necessary for so great a ministry; for perhaps with this spur my great and very great negligence would be excited. This is the principal cause that moves me — while to the prayers and Holy Sacrifices of Your Paternity I commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most humble and unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 19 May 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, May 19, 1642",
  "date_only": "May 19, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 19,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 209,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2826"
 },
 "150": {
  "number": 150,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, May 26, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 373",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although nothing new occurs to me, yet the present shall serve to remind Your Paternity that, when occasion occurs, she wish to remember me, and console me; for as I have written other times, this is the only, and sole consolation that I hope and desire — nor shall I ever hold myself content except when I see myself already to have obtained the intent: of going and shedding my blood and life in service of my Christ the Lord. For love of God I pray Your Paternity that, when occasion should occur, I be included in the number of those who shall go; and albeit by my little virtue I be unworthy of so great a grace, yet the mercy of the Lord shall supply my failing — for if I had not this firm hope in the mercy of God, I would not have dared to ask of Your Paternity this grace. Therefore I pray her anew to remember me, and commend me to the Lord in her Holy prayers and Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 26 May 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, May 26, 1642",
  "date_only": "May 26, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 26,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 211,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2827"
 },
 "151": {
  "number": 151,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, June 16, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 379",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have heard from some letters that have come from your quarters that it will not be long before Your Paternity sends some men to the Indies. With this letter I first remind him of my request, that he would console me. Secondly, I beg him for love of the Lord not to allow others to go while I am not among them, for I would die of holy envy; for I desire to partake of so great a grace, and also because God knows when there will be another such opportunity. All the more so because, as I have always written to Your Paternity, I have placed myself in his hands, to be sent wherever it pleases Your Paternity to send me. I pray, then, with all the affection and earnestness I can muster, that he will not let this opportunity pass — all the more so because the Lord, in His mercy, continually gives me occasions in which I see ever more clearly that this is His most holy will. I trust that He Himself will move Your Paternity's heart to send me, and just as in this mission everything comes from His hand, so I trust that He will arrange and dispose things so that I may be consoled. Meanwhile I beg him to obtain for me from the Lord, by his holy prayers and sacrifices, that I may be a worthy instrument of His glory."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 16 June 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, June 16, 1642",
  "date_only": "June 16, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 212,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2828"
 },
 "152": {
  "number": 152,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, June 30, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 386",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "From Father Provincial I received news that Your Paternity had written me into the book of those who ask to go to the Indies; there is no doubt that this was most welcome to me, but to my desire it seems very little — all the more so since, as I hear, the departure will be this September. Wherefore I entreat Your Paternity for love of the Lord that he would console me; and if with this opportunity he can send me, it would be my greatest consolation, and, as I hope and believe, a thing most welcome to the Lord, who in His mercy calls me to this. Our Father, ever since I began writing to him, I have placed myself in his hands like a piece of wood; I stand waiting for the movement toward whatever place pleases him most, as the Lord shall move his heart. I know that Your Paternity does not lack workers more worthy than I — indeed, in myself I recognize no dignity or merit for such a grace and favor — but I ask it all as a mercy, and solely because I know that Your Paternity is wont to aid those who are most wretched and unworthy, it being the property of Christ to make apostles of persecutors and evangelists of usurers. I beg him for love of the Lord to remember me and to console me as soon as possible, while I commend myself to his holy prayers and sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 30 June 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, June 30, 1642",
  "date_only": "June 30, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 30,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 213,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2829"
 },
 "153": {
  "number": 153,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, July 16, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 393",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "If I have let two posts pass without writing to Your Paternity begging to be sent to the Indies, the cause has not been that my desire is less alive now than before; on the contrary, I feel it kindle in me continually, and the Lord in His mercy gives me new incentives. It was that I might not weary Your Paternity with so many letters, and also because of my extraordinary occupations in preparing for the examination for the fourth year of theology. I say this because I take this occasion to remind him that it is now time, if Your Paternity wishes to console me; for whether wholly or not, little remains before I shall be entirely loosed from my studies, so that I may employ myself in the service of our common Lord. From my Father Rector I received the greeting Your Paternity sent me; but together with it — though most welcome to me — I would have wished some good and near hope of being consoled as soon as possible, all the more so because, as I hear, the departure will be this year. I beg him for love of the Lord to remember me and to console me as soon as possible. I pray the Lord that in His mercy He may move Your Paternity's heart to console me. I commend myself to his holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 16 July 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, July 16, 1642",
  "date_only": "July 16, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 214,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2813"
 },
 "154": {
  "number": 154,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, July 21, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 397",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I humbly thank Your Paternity for the remembrance he keeps of me in his letters to my Father Rector and Father Provincial, but I would that he also remember to console me as soon as possible by sending me to the Indies I so ardently desire. I count the days and hours, and it seems to me they creep by until there comes from Your Paternity the news of departure I desire; with every post I ask what news has come; every letter I believe to be the one that will bear my joy to me, but I always find them empty of it, and I remain with my desire increased. How many times I lament my negligence, for had it not been boundless, I would now be in possession of so great a good! Often I lament the place where I find myself, so far from Your Paternity that two months must pass before I can have any answer. Gladly, if it pleased Your Paternity, I would change places with someone from the Province of Naples — not for any other reason but that I would find myself readier, and on occasion more prompt to carry out whatever might be commanded me by Your Paternity concerning my petition; I have seen by experience that the nearer men are, the more occasions they have to obtain what they holily desire. Secondly, because this would be a greater consolation to me: being far from relatives and acquaintances, I would have more occasion to dedicate myself and give myself wholly to my Lord until I am called by Your Paternity. I have proposed my desire, but I submit myself to whatever Your Paternity commands me, nor do I desire anything but to be handled and moved by holy obedience. I tell Your Paternity in all sincerity that I do not profess these things as ends in themselves, and what I do I do for no other end than to obtain as soon as possible the desired mission; and so I beg him, and for love of the wounds of Christ I entreat him, to console me, and in his holy prayers and sacrifices to pray the Lord for this unworthy son of his in the Lord."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy son and servant — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 21 July 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, July 21, 1642",
  "date_only": "July 21, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 21,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 215,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2814"
 },
 "155": {
  "number": 155,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, August 12, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 400",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "O God, when will that letter come that will bring infinite consolation and joy to my heart! I am confident, trusting in the mercy of the Lord and in his who is my Father in the Lord, that it is to come and will come without doubt; but what I would wish is that it come as soon as possible. Too heavy is the delay for one who greatly desires. Your Paternity knows, as I have written at other times, that my desire is such that every other thing but this can give me not only no solid joy, but not even, as it seems to me, the beginning of it. If, then, I desire this consolation, why should I not obtain it when I ask it of my Father — all the more so since I am most certain that it is his will, and that in everything that can console and promote his subjects and sons in Christ to perfection he is most ready. I pray, then, that Your Paternity, as soon as possible, will deign to remember me and send me where I am called by the Lord. I commend myself to the prayers and holy sacrifices of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 12 August 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, August 12, 1642",
  "date_only": "August 12, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 217,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2815"
 },
 "156": {
  "number": 156,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, August 25, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 401",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Blessed be the Lord God, who has willed to console the whole Society, and not least me his unworthy son, with the recovery of Your Paternity's health; I hope in His infinite mercy that He will grant it completely, for the good of the whole Society — which the whole world knows what a Father it would lose. I render new and infinite thanks to the Lord, and I pray Him to diminish my useless life in order to add it to Your Paternity's. With this occasion I also pray him to remember me and to send me where I am called by God; if Your Paternity knew the immense desire I have in my breast for this mission to the Indies, he would certainly send me at once. I cannot otherwise explain it than by letters. For love of the Lord, let him not forget me in the occasions that arise; and in his holy sacrifices and prayers commend me to the Lord."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 25 August 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, August 25, 1642",
  "date_only": "August 25, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 218,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2816"
 },
 "157": {
  "number": 157,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, September 1, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 402",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "If my sins were not what they are, without doubt the Lord God would have by this hour disposed things so that Your Paternity would soon send more workers than one from Sicily, and I would not stand here with this envy — though I know it is a good one — which I bear toward our Father Metello Saccano, called by Your Paternity for Japan. Ever since I learned of his call, the Lord has made me know more fully how imperfect I am, having seen clearly that those are chosen by Him who render themselves more worthy by the exercise of the virtues. Yet for all this I shall not cease to pray Your Paternity to send me, for I hope in the Lord that He will give me such grace as will supply my defect, since He has called me and continually calls me. Is it possible that I shall not obtain from Your Paternity what I know most certainly He relishes and wishes to grant me? I make no condition about the mission: wherever he sends me I shall go most readily. This I desire, this I long for, this I think on day and night; this I shall ask until either death comes or I am sent by Your Paternity for love of the Lord. I beg him to remember me, and in his holy sacrifices and prayers to commend me to the Lord."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 1 September 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 1, 1642",
  "date_only": "September 1, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 1,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 219,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2817"
 },
 "158": {
  "number": 158,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, September 15, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 411",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Until now I have lived, as the saying goes, on hope, believing that this year I should be consoled by Your Paternity and sent to my Indies; but since it has not pleased the Lord that it be so, may His most holy will be done. At least I pray Your Paternity to assure me that he will send me with the first mission made after this one, and with the first who go. Today, finding myself precisely in the holy Exercises, I was thinking what means I might take to obtain from Your Paternity as soon as possible what I so ardently desire; but finding nothing to lay hold of, I chose — cast at Your Paternity's feet, since it is not lawful for me to do so in person and with the body — to do it with the spirit and the heart, and to beg him for love of the Lord to console me, for otherwise I shall die of pure desire. O how many times have I wished for wings, that I might in person plead my case before Your Paternity and vent to him the ardor I have in my breast! I have placed myself in the hands of holy obedience: what do I desire but that I be set by it, wherever it says and judges? This I ask, this I seek, and either I shall obtain it or die of pure desire. And so I pray him anew, for the love of God, who communicates this desire to me, to console me and to commend me to the Lord in his holy prayers and sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 15 September 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 15, 1642",
  "date_only": "September 15, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 220,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2818"
 },
 "159": {
  "number": 159,
  "byline_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano — Bologna, April 3, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 53",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "On this day, on which the Redeemer of the world offered Himself in a most perfect holocaust to the eternal Father for my sake, I resolve at last to offer myself as a holocaust too, for love of the same Lord, into the hands of Your most Reverend Paternity, for the missions of the Indies or Japan. This desire of mine is not, however, sudden, but very old, although until now it has remained shut up in my heart; for until now I contented myself with being so prepared that, whenever I might have been sent, I would have gone at once without reply. But a stronger impulse has been added to me in these days by hearing, from a letter from the Indies transmitted to a scholastic of this College, of the scarcity of workers to cultivate that new vineyard, nay to gather the already-ripened harvest; whence I have felt myself moved to say to him: Ecce ego, mitte me. (here I am, send me) With this letter, then, I intend nothing other than to signify to him how much it seems to me that God speaks to my heart — submitting myself always, and contenting myself more than with anything else with whatever, high or low, shall be ordered of me by whomsoever. Meanwhile I place the matter in his hands, nor do I wish in any way to depart from his will. Let Your Paternity dispose of me as he judges, shaping me like a pliant rod or soft wax in whatever manner pleases him most; for I wish at all costs to live in every possible dependence on holy obedience. If Your Paternity judges me fit for this trade, behold me attentive to his beck and call; may it please Heaven to supply me with His aid, for then I am ready. In conclusion, I ask of him his holy and paternal blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your most Reverend Paternity, servant and son in Christ — Sebastiano Berni. Bologna, 3 April 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano",
  "place_raw": "Bologna",
  "date_raw": "Bologna, April 3, 1643",
  "date_only": "April 3, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 3,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 221,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2691"
 },
 "160": {
  "number": 160,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, April 6, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 57",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Our Father, on the one hand I have nothing to write except to pray him for what I have continually prayed him; on the other I cannot but write, for so great is the desire I have to obtain this grace from Your Paternity that, were I to let some post pass without writing, it would seem to me I had committed a very great fault, and that I did not correspond to the great benefits of the Lord. Besides, the desire itself gives me no rest unless I pray him continually, as I have done, and more often I would do it if I had more often the opportunity. For love of the Lord, let Your Paternity remember me in the occasions that arise, and console me as soon as the occasion comes; and I hope in the great charity of Your Paternity, which I know rejoices when it gives its sons occasion of consolation. I commend myself to his holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 6 April 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, April 6, 1643",
  "date_only": "April 6, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 6,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 223,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2836"
 },
 "161": {
  "number": 161,
  "byline_raw": "Serafini, Fortunato — Rome (Collegio Germanico), July 11, 1643 (database indexes as April 11, 1643)",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 111",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "To compress into little the much that I must most humbly lay before Your Paternity concerning my vocation to the Indies, I say with all truth that it has lasted the space of twelve continuous years that I have unworthily spent in the Religious state, and has not only never grown lukewarm but has ever been more inflamed, especially on occasions of retreat, whether for the Spiritual Exercises or for the renewal of vows, and at many other times kindled by unusual impulses. To this is added that I have weighed well all the difficulties that might draw me back, but they have served only to confirm me all the more in my resolve — indeed, in the vow lately made with Your Paternity's approval — to seek with the greatest insistence I can the mission to the Indies, where I may scatter, amid a thousand labors in the service of souls, the Gospel and my blood; of which I feel intensely assured, with particular consolation and certain hope of obtaining the grace from Your Paternity in the present occasion of the coming of the Father Procurator of Ethiopia, to whom I have offered myself, and with whom I recognize it to be God's will that I go, without further delay, under pretext of which I shall never allow myself to be induced to desist from the insistence which I feel in obligation to make, that I may correspond to the divine vocation, seeing clearly that by delaying I would run a manifest danger of failing God, not without great reproach on the Day of Judgment; besides which I am so far advanced in age and in my studies that any delay seems to me to be nothing but very harmful to me. In sum, my principal motive, which counts for a thousand, is to do the will of God, of which, as I have never doubted in all this time, I am likewise persuaded that Your Paternity is not to have any doubt; and therefore, setting aside every other consideration to the contrary, I am to be granted the so-much-desired and so-often-requested license, which now anew I request with the last effort of affection and with greater insistence than all those past, so that, fully assured of God's will, I may with all confidence in His help set myself to an enterprise so arduous and so disproportionate to my weakness and unfitness, which is to cooperate in the salvation of so many souls bought with the precious blood of our Lord Jesus Christ. I add no more, for I deem that this is enough to move Your Paternity to cooperate in the most singular grace that God does me, by sending me where He calls me, I promising with His grace never to turn back but to go ever forward unto death, and death of the Cross, for which in conclusion I beg Your Paternity to help me with your Holy Sacrifices and prayers, to which with all my heart I commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most humble servant and most unworthy son — Fortunato Serafini. From the Collegio Germanico, 11 July 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Serafini, Fortunato",
  "place_raw": "Rome (Collegio Germanico)",
  "date_raw": "Rome (Collegio Germanico), July 11, 1643",
  "date_only": "July 11, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 11,
  "date_note": "database indexes as April 11, 1643",
  "book_page_start": 224,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2858"
 },
 "162": {
  "number": 162,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, June 13, 1643 (letter signed June 13, 1642)",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 91",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Iesus Maria (Jesus, Mary)",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "After so many petitions made through all the years I have been in the Company, I now begin to renew them on the occasion of the coming of the Father Procurator of the Indies, Father Francesco Carvaglio. I know that Your Paternity, for the affection you bear me and for your charity, will not have me suffer any more. Truly, the other times I have found myself occupied in one thing or another; now Our Lord has made me quite free, so that in this very moment I could leap to the ship and fly off to where Our Lord calls me. My Father, with infinite tears I throw myself at the feet of Your Paternity and beg you to console me. Send me where for so many years I have sighed, where so many companions call me, where God seems in every way to will me, and where Your Paternity has deigned to destine me, sending me the crucifix as pledge, and with almost infinite promises. May Our Lord grant Your Paternity long life for His glory, and to me only as much as will let me reach the Indies and shed my blood for the faith. I close by begging you to give me your blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, a most useless servant and son in Christ Jesus — Lonardo Cinami. Naples, 13 June 1642 [database indexes as June 13, 1643]."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, June 13, 1643",
  "date_only": "June 13, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 13,
  "date_note": "letter signed June 13, 1642",
  "book_page_start": 226,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2854"
 },
 "163": {
  "number": 163,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, July 25, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 114",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Iesus Maria (Jesus, Mary)",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I return to make known to Your Paternity my supreme and only desire for the Indies, for I know that shortly Your Paternity will make the most fortunate choice of subjects for that most glorious Mission. And though each day I see myself more unworthy of it for my sins, yet I say to Your Paternity what I say to God: Si iniquitates observaveris Domine, domine quis sustinebit? (if thou, Lord, shouldst mark iniquities, Lord, who shall stand?) This is the seventh year in which I never let an occasion pass to do all I can and to take every means to attain what I desire; and what more has Your Paternity to do to see whether this is the will of God who calls me? I renew now, in this last occasion, all my past petitions, and I lay before her all my tears, and I beg you, by that Lord who must judge me, to hear the voices of one who for so many years has cried out continually. I close with Your Paternity, but my tears do not end, which I cast continuously at the feet of the crucifix for this which I call beatitude in this life. Wherewith I beg you to give me your blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, a most unworthy servant and son in Christ Jesus — Lonardo Cinami. Naples, 25 July 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, July 25, 1643",
  "date_only": "July 25, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 227,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2855"
 },
 "164": {
  "number": 164,
  "byline_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano — Bologna, September 9, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 134",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "If ever my soul tasted true gladness, it was when, by Your Paternity's reply received through the Father Rector, I conceived a sure hope that I too must one day follow in the footsteps of those who labor with such zeal for the aid of the Indies. And how often I think of it — I do so very often through the day — I know not what sweetness I feel, which to my mind can be none but of Paradise. I have wished many times to make it known to Your Paternity, and my not having done so was a certain respect which I now condemn. And indeed, to tell the truth, I feel myself stirred by holy emulation of those whom this very day I have heard are already summoned for so fortunate a sending. O Our Father, by me most dearly loved, kneeling as I write, I beg you, as much as Your Paternity has at heart my salvation and that of others, to obtain for me a place among these. Of grace, by the most august memory of the unconquered Martyr Father Carlo Spinola (whose triumph's eve is today), may Your Paternity hearken to so many prayers, for my heart tells me that in this perhaps my predestination is at stake. Already into your hands now (I have already done so many times into the hands of St. Francis Xavier) I renounce Italy, my homeland, and all that might delay my going. Nothing but one nod of yours is lacking to me to set out for another world. What costs Your Paternity so little may buy me Paradise. With all my heart I beseech you, and at the last, prostrate on the ground, I ask your paternal blessing and at once the most desired Mission. Pater mi, ecce ego, mitte me. (my Father, here I am, send me)"
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, a servant and son in Christ — Sebastiano Berni. Bologna, 9 September 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano",
  "place_raw": "Bologna",
  "date_raw": "Bologna, September 9, 1643",
  "date_only": "September 9, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 228,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2695"
 },
 "165": {
  "number": 165,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, October 3, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 142",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Iesus Maria (Jesus, Mary)",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I render more thanks to Your Paternity than I have [...] — you have at last deigned to admit so many of my tears, so many sighs, and so many prayers, telling me that this grace was owed me for the infinite insistence I have made. And I answer that even if truly infinite, it would be little, for the grace I have received has no price that can repay it. It was only the benignity of Your Paternity, and my most holy Father and Apostle of the Indies, St. Francis Xavier, who held in his hands the keys of the treasures of Heaven and opened them and made this great prodigality toward one who, after so many years, ought to have been in hell. I meanwhile pray His Divine Majesty to pay Your Paternity the recompense of so signal a grace, for which not only have I no power to make satisfaction, but I see myself unable even to pay it in words. In prayer, however, I shall never forget so obliging a person who has bound me with so much. And if His Divine Majesty shall deign of His mercy to accept this blood: Your Paternity shall have great part in it. In closing I beg you to give me your blessing and to obtain for me from my Protector St. Francis, with the Indies he has given me, the spirit of an Indian."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, a most useless son and servant in Christ Jesus — Lonardo Cinami, most fortunate Indian. Naples, 3 October 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, October 3, 1643",
  "date_only": "October 3, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 3,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 230,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2856"
 },
 "166": {
  "number": 166,
  "byline_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano — Bologna, October 10, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 150",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have no words to express to you the contentment your words give me when they make me hope more nearly for the fulfillment of what with all affection I desire. I thank you therefore, after God, as the one from whom, after the Lord God, I await my going. I am however stirred by the affection of gratitude I profess to the most benign Lord to make anew this oblation of myself; for on this very day I find He made me born into the world, and tomorrow [He made me] be reborn for His service. Would that I had done it as I was well bound to. Therefore, seeing no better way in which I may in some part acknowledge His mercies than by delivering myself wholly to the same One of whom I am wholly, I make in the hands of Your Paternity a holocaust for this Mission; offering myself wholly to the service of those peoples for love of that God who for me in the most perfect holocaust of the Cross offered Himself. In this, nothing else displeases me than the waiting; but even this becomes easy to me when so it pleases Your Paternity. It is indeed true that I desire to make a vow of it; nevertheless I remit myself in this, as in all things, to the judgment of Your Paternity, to whom I have vowed obedience, and I am so content with it that to execute it I willingly renounce every desire of mine, however ardent. There I beg your paternal blessing, and kneeling I reverence you."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, a servant and son in Christ — Sebastiano Berni. Bologna, 10 October 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano",
  "place_raw": "Bologna",
  "date_raw": "Bologna, October 10, 1643",
  "date_only": "October 10, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 10,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 231,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2696"
 },
 "167": {
  "number": 167,
  "byline_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano — Bologna, November 14, 1643",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 170",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I should deem it resistance to the Holy Spirit if I did not disclose to Your Paternity with all reverence and plainness what He says to my heart, as I think, the same Holy Spirit. I say then that there is no occasion on which, thinking of those peoples who, far from spiritual helps, perish for no other cause than lack of laborers — as the reports and letters that come to us from there cry out — I do not feel myself as if spurred not to delay my going but with all diligence to procure it. And it is said to me, not without great feeling and impulse, the greater the more attentively I consider it: So those souls, redeemed with the same blood as yours, created for the same end as yours, lose a good that has no end only because they have no one to help them, and you with all your comforts both corporal and spiritual [...] Your Paternity — that these calls, heard many times a day, make me cry to God, Mercy, and succor for those miserable peoples; and to dedicate myself wholly to their service, whenever so it pleases Your Paternity, who in God's place governs me. If this is a Divine Vocation and His most holy will, I thank Him for it exceedingly, and for my part I shall put it into execution without any delay, if Your Paternity grants it to me. Therefore I supplicate you in visceribus Jesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to put me in the number of those who are to go; and I protest before the same Lord who maintains me that at Death I would not have to render you account of this, concerning having executed it or not. And I say this because, if God should have elected me, of His infinite mercy, to the aid of those peoples, and if through my negligence they were defrauded of the good and grace of the Lord, and the same Lord of His glory in these souls, I could find nothing that would more burden my conscience. Therefore I beg you, by the charity wherewith God sent His most holy Son for me, to send me for them, if so you judge in Domino (in the Lord), so that the Lord may be glorified, who infirma mundi elegit (he has chosen the weak things of the world), in having given to me these holy sentiments and desires, whose fulfillment I solely and above all things await and crave. In closing I humbly ask your paternal blessing as sure pledge and earnest of the desired Mission."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, a servant and son in Christ — Sebastiano Berni. Bologna, 14 November 1643."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano",
  "place_raw": "Bologna",
  "date_raw": "Bologna, November 14, 1643",
  "date_only": "November 14, 1643",
  "year": 1643,
  "month": 11,
  "day": 14,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 232,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2697"
 },
 "168": {
  "number": 168,
  "byline_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano — Bologna, January 13, 1644",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 744, 209",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although, as I have many times repeated to you, I shall always be more than content with whatever Your Paternity shall ordain of me, nevertheless knowing that without prejudice to such indifference one may make proposals, I confess I could not refrain from writing to you when here in the College I heard news that missions are to be dispatched to the Indies or other countries for the conversion of souls. O, might it ever be possible that among these I were numbered! I do not believe there can be a contentment — I will not say greater, but not even equal or comparable to that which, my heart tells me, I should feel. But when then I heard the news of the martyrs, oh, how I longed not only to go but to have gone long ago! Your Paternity with a single nod can give me more than I know how to ask. Kneeling I beg you that, if so you judge for the greater glory of God, you leave me not behind. Verumtamen non mea, sed tua voluntas fiat. (nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done) I cannot persuade myself that this longing comes from anything but a good Spirit; with all that, I would not be its judge, but remit myself wholly to the judgment of Your Paternity, from whom I pray there may come to me your paternal blessing, full of a true and longed-for desire ever to please the good Jesus, who has accepted me into His Company. I ask you for nothing but what you want of me; I only propose to you that I desire supremely once to be far from homeland and kin and comforts, but in the midst of dangers, crosses, sufferings. So may God will it to be in practice as He grants it in spirit. Once more I ask your blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, a servant and son in Christ — Sebastiano Berni. Bologna, 13 January 1644."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Berni, Sebastiano",
  "place_raw": "Bologna",
  "date_raw": "Bologna, January 13, 1644",
  "date_only": "January 13, 1644",
  "year": 1644,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 13,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 234,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2698"
 },
 "169": {
  "number": 169,
  "byline_raw": "Archinto, Giuseppe — Milan, February 20, 1647",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 745, 28",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "For a long time now the fire has lain hidden under the ashes; but now that so many go to spend their labors, some to Chile, some to Japan — and would God that among these my name might be read — it has grown so that without uncovering it, it would cause no small distress in a soul, it being proper for fire to rise on high, scorning these mortal things. Having suffered much in the novitiate, and this, as I believe, being caused by the change of air from Milan to that of Genoa, they came to the decision to dismiss me from the Company so beloved by me; their designs did not take effect, for my soul, too fervent in its thoughts, they thought perhaps to find a heart of flesh, but found it of diamond, still maintaining the designs of my superiors, the infernal enemy firm; I had recourse to my St. Francis, promising to follow him if he would grant me health; Heaven willed that barely five months should pass before, my former health being confirmed, I made, on the day of Blessed Aloysius three years ago, the vows. The fire has ever lain hidden; but now, Father, it has become so great that it could not wait so long to write four lines wherein it has in part wished to be uncovered. I know I am understood by Your Paternity without my explaining myself in words [...]; but to better obtain the license, I pray Your Paternity to grant me leave to go to Chile. Ah, Father, by the bowels of Christ, do not let me be defrauded of the one hope I hold of obtaining from Your Paternity what I desire; but in case I were not heard, I would pray — but I hope in the charity of Your Paternity and in my St. Francis that I shall obtain the grace I desire — not to speak of it with any person. I am now studying logic. In closing, sure of obtaining the grace, I humbly reverence you."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, a most humble son in the Lord — Gioseppe Archinto. Milan, 20 February 1647."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Archinto, Giuseppe",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, February 20, 1647",
  "date_only": "February 20, 1647",
  "year": 1647,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 20,
  "date_note": null,
